---
/**
 * Privacy policy.
 *
 * Short because the site genuinely does very little. The temptation with a page
 * like this is to copy a generic template full of clauses about marketing
 * preferences and third-party advertising partners that do not exist here.
 * A privacy policy describing processing that does not happen is worse than
 * none: it is the same failure as a citation pointing at the wrong paper.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const updated = "13 September 2026";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy | AI Safety Index"
  description="What this site collects, which is almost nothing, and what the host logs on its behalf."
  cites={[]}
>
  <section class="section section--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="masthead">
        <h1>Privacy</h1>
        <p class="masthead__meta mono">Last updated {updated}</p>
      </div>

      <div class="doc">
        <p class="lede">
          This site sets no cookies, runs no analytics, embeds no third-party scripts and has no
          accounts, forms or comments. It ships no JavaScript at all. There is nothing here that
          tracks you.
        </p>

        <h2>What we collect</h2>
        <p>
          Nothing, directly. There is no mechanism on this site for you to give us information and
          no code on it that gathers any.
        </p>

        <h2>What the host logs</h2>
        <p>
          The site is served by Cloudflare. Like every web host, Cloudflare processes the requests
          it serves in order to serve them and to protect the service from abuse. That normally
          includes your IP address, the page requested, the time, and the user agent your browser
          sends. This happens at the infrastructure layer, we do not add to it, and we do not
          build profiles from it.
        </p>
        <p>
          What Cloudflare retains, and for how long, is governed by
          <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare's own privacy policy</a>
          rather than by this one.
        </p>

        <h2>What your browser stores</h2>
        <p>
          Nothing that we put there. No cookies, no local storage, no fingerprinting. Your browser
          will cache the page and its fonts the way it caches any site, which is a browser feature
          rather than something we set.
        </p>

        <h2>Fonts and other assets</h2>
        <p>
          Fonts are served from this site, not from a font network, so loading a page does not
          send a request to Google or anyone else. The site deliberately carries no images, no
          embedded video, no social widgets and no tag managers. Every byte a page loads comes
          from this domain.
        </p>

        <h2>Links out</h2>
        <p>
          This site is an index, so nearly every page links to other people's. Following one of
          those links takes you somewhere with its own privacy practices, which are theirs and not
          ours. Links to sources are plain links with no tracking parameters attached.
        </p>

        <h2>Data about other people</h2>
        <p>
          The data published here describes countries, organisations, models, laws and
          aggregate populations. Individuals appear only where they are already named in a public
          record we are citing, such as the author of a paper or the publisher of a government
          document. We hold no personal data about readers and do not seek any.
        </p>

        <h2>Your rights</h2>
        <p>
          Australian privacy law and the GDPR both give people rights over personal information
          held about them. We hold none, so in practice there is nothing for us to give you access
          to, correct or erase. If you believe that is wrong, or if something published here
          concerns you personally, <a href="/corrections/">tell us</a> and we will deal with it.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes</h2>
        <p>
          If this ever changes, it will change here first, and the date at the top will move. The
          site's full history is public, so any change to this page is visible in the repository
          as a dated commit rather than a silent edit.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
